what is scheduling ?
 - scheduling in spring boot allows yout to automate tasks at specific intervals or times.
 - it help in running background jobs without manual intervention.

why do we need scheduling?
- without scheduling, repetitive tasks must be triggered manually.



use case of scheduling?
| **Scenario**                      | **How Scheduling Helps**                                                   |
| --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| order processing                  | Automatically process pending orders every 5 minutes                       |
| email notifications               | Send promotional emails daily at midnight                                  |
| data cleanup                      | Delete old records every sunday at 2 AM                                    |
| stock updates                     | fetch stock prices  from APIs every hour                                   |
| system health check               | log system status every minute                                             |


How to enable scheduling in spring boot?
use in main class / configuration class  @EnableScheduling


@@Scheduled :
  - it is used to run a method automatically at a fixed time interval or specific time, without manually calling it.
  - this annotation has various attributes :
                  | Attribute         | Purpose                                                    |
                  | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
                  | `cron`            | Runs task using cron expression                            |
                  | `zone`            | Sets timezone for cron execution                           |
                  | `fixedRate`       | Runs task at fixed interval (based on start time)          |
                  | `fixedRateString` | Same as fixedRate but as String (supports external config) |
                  | `fixedDelay`      | Runs task after last method execution if any method calls there |


Cron expression :  Cron expression gives more control over scheduling.
┌───────────── second (0 - 59)
│ ┌─────────── minute (0 - 59)
│ │ ┌───────── hour (0 - 23)
│ │ │ ┌─────── day of month (1 - 31)
│ │ │ │ ┌───── month (1 - 12)
│ │ │ │ │ ┌─── day of week (0 - 7) (Sun=0 or 7)
│ │ │ │ │ │
* * * * * *


Cron Special Characters Table :
| Character | Meaning           | Usage                                               | Example             | Example Meaning              |
| --------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| `*`       | Any value         | Matches all possible values                         | `0 * * * * ?`       | Every minute                 |
| `?`       | No specific value | Used in day-of-month or day-of-week (one must be ?) | `0 0 10 ? * MON`    | Every Monday at 10 AM        |
| `-`       | Range             | Defines range of values                             | `0 0 9-17 * * ?`    | Every hour from 9 AM to 5 PM |
| `,`       | List              | Multiple specific values                            | `0 0 9,12,15 * * ?` | At 9 AM, 12 PM, and 3 PM     |
| `/`       | Step / Increment  | Run at intervals                                    | `0 0/5 * * * ?`     | Every 5 minutes              |
| `L`       | Last              | Last day of month/week                              | `0 0 10 L * ?`      | Last day of month at 10 AM   |
| `W`       | Weekday           | Nearest weekday                                     | `0 0 10 15W * ?`    | Nearest weekday to 15th      |
| `#`       | Nth weekday       | Specific occurrence of weekday                      | `0 0 10 ? * MON#2`  | Second Monday of month       |
Note:- instead of remembering this table search online croneMaker.



